                <head>269. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>On November 25, President Eisenhower sent a message to Guinean President Sékou
                    Touré replying to a November 20 cable from Touré. In that message, Touré charged
                    that the United States had taken a “partisan position” and was supporting “the
                    enemies of African emancipation” in the Congo and threatened that if the United
                    Stated maintained its position, Guinea would refuse to take part in the
                    Conciliation Commission and would take “any position in African affairs
                    consistent with Congolese interests.” President Eisenhower’s reply declared that
                    the United States had “been in the forefront of those nations who have favored
                    emancipation of all peoples” and urged Guinean support to the U.N. effort in the
                    Congo. For texts of both messages, see <hi rendition="#italic">American Foreign
                        Policy: Current Documents, 1960,</hi> pages 601–602.</p>
            </div>
